    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "i'm having some trouble with some walnut joinery. i'm cutting dovetails but the tips of the pins keep splintering or tearing out slightly. what am I doing wrong?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That is a very common issue, but it is often caused by the wood being too dry or the saw blade not being sharp enough to slice cleanly through the grain. When you cut dovetails, you are essentially trying to slice a wedge of wood; if the blade is dull, it acts like a sawing motion that tears the fibers rather than cutting them, which is exactly what causes those splintered tips.\n\nTo fix this, you should first check your blade and ensure it is sharp, as a dull blade is the most frequent culprit for this kind of \"gummy\" tear-out. Additionally, make sure your wood is properly conditioned; if the walnut is too dry, it will shrink and crack when you try to cut it, so soaking it or using a moisture conditioner can help stabilize the fibers.\n\nYou might also consider using a sharp chisel to clean up the corners after sawing, as this helps to remove any residual tear-out that the saw couldn't catch. If you are using a router, ensure your bit is sharp and the depth is set correctly to avoid burning the wood or causing the bit to grab and rip the material.\n\nThink of the wood fibers like a bundle of stiff grass; if you try to cut",
