    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "the grass analogy makes sense. but even with a really sharp chisel, it feels like the wood is fighting me at the very last second when i'm paring the pins. almost like the grain is being stubborn right at the point of contact. is there anything else i should be checking with the grain direction or the way i'm holding the chisel?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That \"stubbornness\" at the very last second is a classic sign of the wood fibers trying to snap back before you've fully committed the cut, often because the chisel angle isn't perfectly perpendicular to the grain at that critical moment. When you are paring the pins, you are essentially trying to separate the wood fibers from the pin tip; if the grain runs slightly off at an angle, the chisel can catch and tear rather than slice.\n\nYou should double-check that your chisel is held at a perfect 90-degree angle to the face of the wood, not just to the edge of the pin. Even a slight deviation can cause the blade to dig in and rip the walnut fibers, especially in hard woods like walnut which are notorious for being \"gummy\" when they are stressed.\n\nAlso, consider the direction of your push; you want to push the chisel away from you, letting the sharp edge do the work, rather than pulling it through the wood. If you pull, you are dragging the dull side of the blade across the grain, which is like trying to run through deep mud with your feet dragging behind you instead of pushing forward.\n\nSometimes, the issue is simply that the wood has been",
