    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That inconsistency is the hallmark of a \"hidden gnat\" in the wood, or what we call a \"ghost knot\" that hasn't fully formed yet. Walnut is notorious for having these microscopic voids or early-stage knots that look like clear grain but are actually weak points waiting to be triggered by a sharp chisel. When you hit one, it's like the wood is suddenly losing its structural integrity, and the pin tip pops out because the fibers behind it have already been compromised by a tiny, invisible defect.\n\nYou should look closely at the cross-section of the wood before you even start cutting; sometimes a \"goblin's eye\" or a small, dark streak running parallel to the grain can indicate where these weak spots are hiding. If you see any faint lines or color variations in the grain, treat that area as a \"no-go zone\" and adjust your cut slightly away from it.\n\nAnother possibility is that the wood has been stressed or dried unevenly, creating internal tension that releases right when you apply pressure. This is similar to a goblin trying to burst out of a box; the pressure builds up until the moment of contact, and then it snaps. To mitigate this, try to plane the surface to a uniform",
